{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"At least 512 MB of compressed in-memory swap is active on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct observations before and after the persistence restart show /dev/zram0 active as swap, backed by a 536,870,912-byte zram device using the lzo compressor. The reported usable swap is 524,284 KiB, exceeding 512 decimal MB and reflecting only the normal swap-header reduction from the configured 512 MiB device.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3fc6f36ce34f498e","cmd-80a02ba6f2cb425f"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"The compressed swap is activated automatically at boot.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A healthy pre-restart baseline showed the service enabled and active. A bounded restart was acknowledged, the node became temporarily unreachable, its boot ID changed, and the recovered system again showed the service enabled and active with /dev/zram0 mounted as swap.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3fc6f36ce34f498e","cmd-44b9254557994bd2","cmd-f52d221726e94442","cmd-288ea08f9c364d93","cmd-80a02ba6f2cb425f","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"The in-memory swap is preferred over any disk-backed swap.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The live zram swap has priority 100, the persistent startup command configures that priority, and both pre- and post-restart checks found no active non-zram swap.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3fc6f36ce34f498e","cmd-80a02ba6f2cb425f"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Creating the service and scripts, refreshing package metadata, installing the available kernel/module packages, selecting that kernel, and performing the two restarts all had clear causal roles in supplying the missing zram module, completing the task, or validating boot persistence. The briefly failed service start exposed the missing module and was corrected; the intermediate malformed legacy-GRUB default was immediately repaired. Final observations show no failed services, package inconsistency, new listening sockets, or abandoned temporary artifacts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3e9bf2bb11ae4bd4","cmd-35535f27554b4b02","cmd-cd09089c6af54885","cmd-fc5fb93d01bd4a9a","cmd-97168d668ac04c13","cmd-6dc59e7862954f1d","cmd-f934bad333fc42cd","cmd-44b9254557994bd2","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. Node1 has persistent, lzo-compressed 512 MiB zram swap at priority 100, with no disk-backed swap active. The mandatory post-completion restart protocol is coherent: healthy baseline, restart acknowledgement and disruption, changed boot identity, and recovered node and zram service. The fixed final boot identity agrees with the executor's post-restart observation.","limitations":["The lifecycle snapshots do not themselves inventory the zram service or installed packages in detail; those outcomes rely on direct executor commands. The global snapshot nevertheless independently confirms the final boot identity, kernel, package-manager consistency, and absence of failed services.","There was no application data or multi-node cluster state in this standalone task to validate beyond node health and the configured zram service."]}
